NAME
====

**lg** - convert between markup and Hiccup data structures

SYNOPSIS
========

**lg** [**-f** _format_ | **--format** _format_] [**-i** _number_ |
       **--indent** _number_] [**-o** _path_ | **--output** _path_] [**-p** |
       **--pretty**] [**-r** | **--reverse**] [**-w** _number_ |
       **--width** _number_] [_input_]

DESCRIPTION
===========

**lg** converts a markup language like HTML or XML into a __Hiccup__ data
structure, which it writes out as Janet source. With **--reverse**, the
conversion runs the other way and a Hiccup data structure is converted into
markup.

Input is read from the file at _input_, or from stdin if _input_ is not given.
Output is written to stdout unless **--output** names a file.

Output is written on a single line unless **--pretty** is given. The options
that shape the layout, **--indent** and **--width**, have no effect without it.
More information is set out in `<Pretty Printing>` below.

Options
-------

- **-f** _format_ | **--format** _format_:
  The _format_ of the markup, either ``html`` or ``xml``. The default is
  ``html``. In HTML, the elements that cannot have children are fixed by the
  language and are written without a closing tag. In XML, nothing is void and
  an element with no children closes itself.

- **-i** _number_ | **--indent** _number_:
  The _number_ of spaces added for each level of nesting when pretty printing.
  The default is 2. This applies whichever way the conversion runs.

- **-o** _path_ | **--output** _path_:
  Writes the output to the file at _path_. By default, the output is written to
  stdout.

- **-p** | **--pretty**:
  Pretty prints the output over multiple lines. By default, the output is
  written on a single line.

- **-r** | **--reverse**:
  Reverses the polarity and converts from Hiccup to markup. The input is read
  as the Janet source of a Hiccup data structure rather than as markup. See
  `<CAVEATS>` below.

- **-w** _number_ | **--width** _number_:
  The _number_ of columns an element is fitted within before it is broken open
  when pretty printing. The default is 80. Only Hiccup output is fitted to a
  width, so this option is ignored when **--reverse** is given.

Hiccup Syntax
-------------

__Hiccup__ represents an element as an array whose first value is a keyword
naming the element. An optional table of attributes may follow, and everything
after that is a child of the element. So the markup:

````
<h1 class="title">Hello <em>world</em></h1>
````

is represented as:

````
@[:h1 @{:class "title"} "Hello " @[:em "world"]]
````

Pretty Printing
---------------

When markup is converted to Hiccup, an element is kept on one line where it
fits within **--width** columns and none of its children starts a block of its
own. Otherwise its children are placed beneath it, each indented by a further
**--indent** spaces. The shape of the source therefore follows the shape of the
markup it stands for.

When Hiccup is converted to markup, a newline is only introduced between two
nodes where it cannot change how the markup is interpreted. The contents of
elements like ``<p>`` and ``<pre>`` are left alone, so the pretty printed markup
renders the same as the markup written on a single line.

EXAMPLES
========

To convert a file of HTML to a Hiccup data structure:

````
$ lg page.html
````

To read from stdin and write the result to a file:

````
$ curl -s https://example.org | lg -o page.janet
````

To pretty print the result, fitting elements within 40 columns and indenting
each level of nesting by 4 spaces:

````
$ lg -p -w 40 -i 4 page.html
````

To convert a Hiccup data structure back to HTML:

````
$ lg -r page.janet
````

To convert a file of XML:

````
$ lg -f xml doc.xml
````

CAVEATS
=======

With **--reverse**, the input is evaluated as Janet source in order to produce
the data structure that is converted. Any code in the input is therefore run.
Do not pass input from an untrusted source to **lg** with **--reverse**.

